Interface is like a blueprint

In Java:
Interface → defines methods
Class → gives implementation

Interfaces are mainly used for:
--> Achieving abstraction
--> Achieving multiple inheritance
--> Making code more flexible and reusable

Syntax
interface Animal 
{
    void sound();
}

--> Animal is an interface
--> sound() has no body
--> Any class using this interface MUST define sound()

Implementing Interface :

interface Animal 
{
    void sound();
}

class Dog implements Animal 
{
    public void sound() 
    {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

public class Main 
{
    public static void main(String[] args) 
    {
        Dog d = new Dog();
        d.sound();
    }
}

Output :
Dog barks

Steps :
1. Interface Created
interface Animal
2. Method Declared
void sound();
3. Class Uses Interface
class Dog implements Animal
4. Method Must Be Written
public void sound()

Difference Between Class and Interface :

| Class                        | Interface                     |
| ---------------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| Can contain complete methods | Mostly method declarations    |
| Uses `extends`               | Uses `implements`             |
| Supports single inheritance  | Supports multiple inheritance |
| Can create objects           | Cannot create objects         |

Simple Analogy :
In real life Java, Rules book is Interface,  
Worker following rules is Class and
Rules	are Methods.

Multiple Inheritance Using Interface :
important -- Java does NOT support multiple inheritance with classes.
             But it supports it using interfaces.

interface A 
{
    void show();
}

interface B 
{
    void display();
}

class Test implements A, B 
{
    public void show() 
    {
        System.out.println("Show");
    }
    public void display() 
    {
        System.out.println("Display");
    }
}

